{"name":"Clytemnestra","hex":"#e8e2e0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Clytemnestra","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A genus of copepods, crustaceans, variously placed:\nA taxonomic genus within the family Peltidiidae.\nA taxonomic genus within the family Clytemnestridae.","A taxonomic genus within the family Peltidiidae."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["wife of Agamemnon, whom she killed after the Trojan War, whereupon was killed herself by their son Orestes"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Clytemnestra"}],"entries":[{"title":"Clytemnestra","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Clytemnestra is the wife of Agamemnon, king of Mycenae, and the half-sister of Helen of Troy. With Agamemnon, she is the mother of Iphigenia, Orestes, Electra, and Chrysothemis. She appears as a character in multiple ancient Greek works, including the Homeric epics and plays by Aeschylus, Sophocles, and Euripides. She is infamous for murdering Agamemnon and the Trojan princess Cassandra, whom Agamemnon had taken as a war prize, when they returned from the Trojan War. In Homer's Iliad, her role in Agamemnon's death is unclear and her character is significantly more subdued. However, in the Odyssey, Agamemnon's ghost tells Odysseus about Clytemnestra's role in his brutal murder."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Clytemnestra_by_John_Collier%2C_1882.jpg/330px-Clytemnestra_by_John_Collier%2C_1882.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clytemnestra"},{"title":"Clytemnestra (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Clytemnestra is a 2023 novel, the debut novel by Italian author Costanza Casati. It is a feminist retelling of the story of Clytemnestra, a character from Greek mythology. The original Clytemnestra has been maligned for her betrayal and murder of her husband Agamemnon. Casati's novel tells Clytemnestra's story from her own perspective."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/92/Clytemnestra_by_Constanza_Casati.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clytemnestra_(novel)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg/500px-Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg/1280px-Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg/500px-Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An already grown-up Clytemnestra (left) watches the egg containing Helen about to hatch on an Athenian red-figure cup by the Xenotimos Painter, c. 430–420 BC, Museum of Fine Arts, Boston.[13]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_(Boston_MFA_99.539).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Murder_of_agamemnon.jpg/500px-Murder_of_agamemnon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Murder_of_agamemnon.jpg/1280px-Murder_of_agamemnon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Murder_of_agamemnon.jpg/500px-Murder_of_agamemnon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Murder of Agamemnon, painting by Pierre-Narcisse Guérin (1817)","link":"https://en.wikipe